  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 AM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThe weather has been rather active over the past couple of days, \nwith some reports of flooding with the monsoonal moisture over the \narea. It also brought some needed rain to portions of the area. And \nthis looks to continue, although there will be some fluctuations \nover the next few days. As for now, we still have some showers and \nembedded thunder out there right now, although they are slowly \nweakening. A few showers may hang around until sunrise though.\n\nWe will still be under the monsoonal moisture today. However, the \ncore of it will shift a bit more to the north and east as some drier \nair tries to punch in from the southwest. Precipitable water values \non average are around 15 to 20 percent lower than on Friday, with a \nnortheast to southwest gradient from most moist to the least (from \naround a 1 in 6 chance in southern Wyoming to as much as 1 out of 2 \nin the northern mountains. So, the best chance of showers and storms \nwill be across northern Wyoming where we still have a marginal risk \nof excessive rainfall today. There is still some steering flow so \nthis should cut the threat of flooding somewhat but it is still \npossible in local areas. Temperatures will be similar to yesterday, \nevening around 5 degrees below seasonal averages. Most guidance is \nalso showing an earlier show, with most convection over by sunset \nand almost all showers and storms over by midnight.\n\nDrier air should then push further eastward on Sunday, dropping the \nchance of showers and storms even more. There will still be a few \naround, but with precipitable water values returning close to \nseasonal normals and dew points generally 10 to 15 degrees lower \nthan on Saturday, most locations should be rain free. There will \nalso be more sunshine along with somewhat warmer temperatures aloft. \nThis will allow for warmer temperatures, probably returning closer \nto seasonal normals. At this point, things look largely quiet after \nsunset on Sunday night.\n\nThings may turn more active again on Monday, especially later and \ninto the night. Guidance shows precipitable water values climbing \nback to around 130 to 150 percent of normal. At the same time,a \nsubtle shortwave will be moving through the area. As a result, the \nchance of showers and storms increases again this day, and possibly \nlasting into the nighttime hours as well. This more active pattern \nlooks to last into Tuesday and possible Wednesday as well, \nespecially across northern Wyoming where another shortwave may be \npassing. Temperatures through this period look to be near to \npossibly slightly below normal. \n\nFollowing Wednesday, most guidance shows ridging over the desert \nsouthwest building back northward into the central and northern \nRockies for the end of the work week. As a result, temperatures \nshould begin a warming trend starting on Thursday and especially \nFriday and into next weekend, when above normal temperatures again \nlook likely for most of the area, with 90s across many of the lower \nelevations East of the Divide. As for convection, Thursday and \nFriday look largely dry at this point, with only isolated showers \nover the higher elevations. Some monsoonal moisture may return for \nthe weekend, but this far out it uncertain is rather high. So, the \nstory for the next seven days will be the monsoon, and it's ebbs and \nflows over the period.",
